The dereference construct `@ Exp` of FUN has no symbol name of its own, so
kompile names its symbol and the rule labels of its heating and cooling rules
after the syntax, `@__FUN-UNTYPED-COMMON`. The LLVM backend puts a rule label
as it is in the name of a string constant, and a linker reads the `@` in a
symbol name as a version mark (`name@VERSION`), so the shared library the tower
needs (`llvm-kompile ... c`) does not link: `symbol str_lit_FUN-UNTYPED-COMMON.@...
has undefined version`. Naming the symbol `deref` keeps the `@` out of every
label; nothing else changes.

--- a/fun-untyped.md
+++ b/fun-untyped.md
@@ -291,7 +291,7 @@
 ```k
   syntax Exp ::= "ref"                             [macro]
                | "&" Name
-               | "@" Exp                                     [strict]
+               | "@" Exp                                     [strict, symbol(deref)]
                | Exp ":=" Exp                                [strict]
                | Exp ";" Exp                       [strict(1), right]
 
@@ -363,7 +363,7 @@
 ## Additional Priorities
 
 ```k
-  syntax priority @__FUN-UNTYPED-COMMON
+  syntax priority deref
                 > apply
                 > arith
                 > _:=__FUN-UNTYPED-COMMON
